# Desde Viewer — local configuration template.
#
# Every variable below is optional. The viewer runs with none of them set:
# `npm run dev` seeds a demo project and prints a one-time local sign-in
# link, with zero configuration. You only need this file for GitHub
# sign-in and repo connection, SMTP mention emails, or a deployment
# reachable by people other than you.
#
# Copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) and fill in. Run with:
#   npm run dev:local
#
# Specific SUBGROUPS below are ALL-OR-NOTHING, not the file as a whole: the
# sign-in duo (CLIENT_ID/CLIENT_SECRET), the repositories trio
# (APP_ID/APP_PRIVATE_KEY/APP_SLUG) — independent of the sign-in duo, see
# the comment where it starts below — and the SMTP quad
# (HOST/USER/PASS/FROM). Setting any one member of one of these groups makes
# the rest of THAT group required, and a missing one throws at boot rather
# than starting half configured. That is deliberate — a viewer that boots
# with broken auth or broken mail is worse than one that refuses to boot.
# The "Core" vars and the standalone "Optional" flags are each independent —
# none of them require any other var to be set.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Core
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PORT=3100
# Must match the GitHub App's callback URL host/port exactly, or the OAuth
# redirect will not come back.
VIEWER_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3100
VIEWER_DATA_DIR=.desde-viewer

# Only "selfhost" (SQLite + local disk) ships today; the type is a union of
# one on purpose. Leave unset — this exists so a second profile has a place
# to plug in later without becoming dead config in the meantime.
# VIEWER_PROFILE=selfhost

# Seeds a demo project at /p/demo/ on a genuinely empty first boot. Set to
# "off" to skip it. Rebuild the fixture itself with `npm run build:demo`.
# VIEWER_DEMO_PROJECT=off

# HMAC key for signing the session cookie. Usually unset — generated for
# you on first boot and stored in $VIEWER_DATA_DIR/config.json (see
# README, "The runtime config file"). Set your own to force a rotation:
# every existing session becomes invalid the moment you do.
# VIEWER_SESSION_SECRET=

# Bearer token for write endpoints. Unset does NOT disable writes — a
# signed-in user can still mint their own write-scoped personal access token
# at /settings. Treat this token as equivalent to the App private key: its
# holder bypasses the per-caller GitHub installation filter by design (see
# README, "Who can sign in determines who can connect a repo").
VIEWER_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# Dev-only Next.js bundler. Default is turbopack; switch to webpack if the
# dev server crashes with a Turbopack symlink error (the tell: your
# project's node_modules resolves through a symlink outside the project
# root, e.g. a shared checkout / git worktree).
# VIEWER_DEV_BUNDLER=turbopack

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub App — sign-in AND repositories (Phase 3c-1b unified these)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE GitHub App provides both. CLIENT_ID/CLIENT_SECRET come from the App's
# own settings page — NOT from a standalone OAuth App. An upgrade that leaves
# old OAuth App values here will look configured and behave strangely.
#
# You don't have to fill these in by hand: the /setup page in a running
# viewer can create the App for you with one click (a GitHub App Manifest),
# and store the credentials it gets back on disk instead. Use the variables
# below only for the manual path — GitHub Enterprise Server, a locked-down
# org, or a deployment where you want these fixed by env rather than by the
# runtime config file. See README, "Registering the App by hand".

VIEWER_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
VIEWER_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

VIEWER_GITHUB_APP_ID=
# The App's URL slug, from https://github.com/apps/<slug>
VIEWER_GITHUB_APP_SLUG=
# The downloaded .pem, base64-encoded so it survives as a single line:
#   base64 -i ~/Downloads/your-app.private-key.pem | tr -d '\n'
# (A literal multi-line PEM is also accepted; base64 is just less painful.)
VIEWER_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Optional, independent of the repositories trio above — only needed for
# push-triggered auto-deploy (see README, "Push-webhook auto-deploy"). Any
# long random string; set the SAME value as the App's own webhook secret.
# Unset means POST /api/v1/webhooks/github answers 503 and never processes
# an unverified payload.
# VIEWER_GITHUB_APP_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# GitHub Enterprise only — leave unset for github.com.
# VIEWER_GITHUB_API_BASE_URL=
# VIEWER_GITHUB_AUTHORIZE_URL=
# VIEWER_GITHUB_TOKEN_URL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mention email. Setting VIEWER_SMTP_HOST makes USER/PASS/FROM required.
# VIEWER_SMTP_HOST=
# VIEWER_SMTP_PORT=587
# VIEWER_SMTP_USER=
# VIEWER_SMTP_PASS=
# VIEWER_SMTP_FROM=
# VIEWER_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET=

# Escape hatch. Disabling the prototype CSP lets a hosted prototype mint a
# personal access token as the signed-in reviewer — a credential that
# survives logout. Only ever for local debugging.
# VIEWER_PROTOTYPE_CSP=off

# Seed sign-in domain rules, on FIRST boot only. Comma-separated domains;
# each becomes a stored domain rule admitting sign-ins from that domain at
# the `viewer` role. After that one boot the variable is ignored: an Admin
# manages domain rules from Settings, and the viewer is invite-only
# regardless (an unknown sign-in with no invite and no matching rule is
# refused). Exact addresses (`person@example.com`) are NOT converted; they
# are skipped with a boot warning, and an invite is the replacement.
# VIEWER_ALLOWED_EMAIL_DOMAINS=example.com,other-team.example

# Full origin isolation: serve prototypes at {slug}.{domain}/ (the origin
# root) instead of (additionally to) /p/{slug}/. Needs wildcard DNS and a
# matching wildcard TLS cert for {domain}. See README, "Subdomain serving".
# VIEWER_SERVE_DOMAIN=proto.example.com

# Weaker origin isolation, for a deployment that can add ONE extra DNS name
# and cert SAN but not a wildcard: a single alternate origin that serves ALL
# prototypes under /p/{slug}/. Every prototype shares that one origin (one
# cookie jar, shared storage), so prefer VIEWER_SERVE_DOMAIN when wildcard
# DNS is available. Must use the same scheme as VIEWER_PUBLIC_URL and live
# on a different registrable domain than it.
# VIEWER_PROTOTYPE_ORIGIN=https://prototypes.example.net

# How many reverse-proxy hops sit in front of this server, or which proxy
# addresses to trust. Unset means nothing is in front of us.
#
# SET THIS IF YOU TERMINATE TLS AT A REVERSE PROXY. Rate limiting keys every
# bucket on the client address, and with this unset every request behind a
# proxy keys to the PROXY's address instead: the per-IP limits collapse into
# one global bucket, so a single abusive visitor exhausts the budget for
# everybody else.
#
# `true` is REFUSED and the boot fails. It makes Express trust the leftmost
# X-Forwarded-For entry, which the client supplies, so an attacker rotates the
# header and defeats rate limiting completely. That is worse than the shared
# bucket this setting exists to fix. Setting a hop count when there is NO proxy
# has the same effect, which is why the default is off.
#
# One proxy (nginx, Caddy, a cloud load balancer): 1
# VIEWER_TRUST_PROXY=1
# Or name the proxies instead:
# VIEWER_TRUST_PROXY=10.0.0.0/8,127.0.0.1
